salary ranges have come to New York City  job postings this month a new salary  transparency law took effect that  requires most employers to provide good  faith salary ranges on job posts the  significance of all of this is huge pay  information has been secret for decades  but since the legislation went into  effect job Seekers are finding that some  companies are sidestepping the new  regulation by listing salary ranges so  wide that they stretch hundreds of  thousands of dollars so does this  loophole mean New York City's new law is  ineffective  pay transparency laws exist to different  degrees in some states in the U.S but  Colorado and now New York City stand out  in that they require this information in  job postings experts say New York City's  salary disclosure law may be especially  impactful because of the size of the  city's economy and the number of  companies there New York City is now the  biggest job market in the U.S to require  employers to list pay ranges and job ads  this law has National implications  because if a job could conceivably be  done in New York employers would have to  comply with it and that applies to  remote roles as well and already we're  starting to see companies across the U.S  begin to list pay for remote roles  wanting to comply with the New York  legislation officials say the New York  City law helps eliminate inherent bias  in setting employee salaries last year  the Pew Research Center found that women  earned around 84 percent of what men  earned in 2020 that Gap has narrowed  significantly from 40 years ago when  women earned a around 64 percent of what  men did lawmakers and other supporters  hope laws like this will lead to more  Equitable workplaces will lead to more  fairness in the process and will help to  sort of level the playing field really  from the moment someone gets hired New  York's law did face pushback from  prominent business groups like The  partnership for New York City which said  the legislation was overly burdensome  for employers who needed more time to  comply with the new rules ultimately the  city did give companies more time and  the city pushed back the implementation  of a law from May until November of this  year right now the partnership for New  York City said most employers are on  board with this they may not be thrilled  about it but they're largely compliant  since the transparency law went into  effect companies have pursued a variety  of approaches to disclosure some  companies still aren't complying at all  others are posting really broad ranges  some companies have had ranges That Vary  only by five thousand dollars and so  it's a really mixed picture so far the  the roles that have attracted the most  attention are those with wide salary  ranges a trauma surgeon in the Mount  Sinai health system could earn anywhere  from three hundred and eighty four  thousand dollars to eight hundred  thousand dollars or more a tax executive  at pricewaterhousecoopers could make 158  thousand dollars or nearly triple that a  radiologist's position at another Mount  Sinai Hospital listed a job that could  pay as much as 525  000 or roughly half that amount at two  hundred and sixty thousand dollars one  of the really fuzzy parts of this law is  what constitutes a good faith range  because employers have made the case  that sometimes ranges need to be super  broad because they don't know who they  want to recruit for a role job Seekers  have said that more specific ranges are  more helpful the agency tasks with  enforcing the law is New York City's  Commission on human rights which will  rely mostly on citizen complaints to  investigate companies that post jobs  without the pay range listed it's  unclear whether the city's Commission on  human rights will will consider the  broad salary ranges a good faith attempt  to post salaries one co-sponsor of the  latest legislation told me that there  may need to be additional legislation  quote soonish to address 